WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.594
Under the old model, a
significant change to a

2
00:00:02.594 --> 00:00:05.412
certified service went
back through an approval.

3
00:00:05.412 --> 00:00:09.849
The Significant Change Notification
rules replace that with notification,

4
00:00:09.849 --> 00:00:12.899
scaled to risk, and the
deadlines are in business days.

5
00:00:12.899 --> 00:00:15.962
The provider classifies
every potential change.

6
00:00:15.962 --> 00:00:19.534
A change of certification
class needs a new assessment;

7
00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:21.863
it can't be handled as a notification.

8
00:00:21.863 --> 00:00:26.009
Routine recurring changes, such as
patching and signature updates,

9
00:00:26.009 --> 00:00:27.638
get no formal notice at all.

10
00:00:27.638 --> 00:00:31.987
Adaptive changes are notified within
10 business days after they finish.

11
00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:35.748
Transformative changes, the
ones with substantive new risk,

12
00:00:35.748 --> 00:00:39.733
need initial plans at least 30
business days before work starts,

13
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:41.989
final plans 10 business days before,

14
00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:46.726
and notices within 5 business days
after finishing and after verification.

15
00:00:46.726 --> 00:00:52.075
Every notice must be human-readable and
JSON, and providers keep 12 months of them.

16
00:00:52.075 --> 00:00:53.950
Evidence becomes a feed too.

17
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.365
Providers must use a
FedRAMP-compatible trust center,

18
00:00:57.365 --> 00:01:01.463
with documented programmatic
access to all certification data.

19
00:01:01.463 --> 00:01:09.700
On October 21, 2026, only 110 of 781
Marketplace listings had a trust-center entry.

20
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.600
There are two consequences for you.

21
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.169
A provider that ships continuously can
treat most releases as routine and file

22
00:01:16.169 --> 00:01:18.869
nothing, so keeping
current with commercial no

23
00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:21.500
longer costs the provider
a compliance cycle.

24
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.837
But your review duty doesn't shrink.

25
00:01:23.837 --> 00:01:27.519
Agencies must review each
Ongoing Certification Report for

26
00:01:27.519 --> 00:01:31.488
changes that affect the risk
accepted in their own authorization.

27
00:01:31.488 --> 00:01:33.037
So name the reviewer now.

28
00:01:33.037 --> 00:01:38.130
FedRAMP recommends designating a federal
senior information security official;

29
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.599
in the program's model that's
the CISO or a named delegate.

30
00:01:41.599 --> 00:01:45.268
The ISSO reads each notice
against the boundary record,

31
00:01:45.268 --> 00:01:50.280
and the authorizing official decides
whether it changes the agency's risk.
